WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.620
Thanks for tuning in to SpaceX Daily, episode 16, it's June 27th, 2026, and I'm Patrick

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.280
in Vancouver.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.080
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:15.320
Ship 40's single engine static fire clears the first hot fire gate for the vehicle that

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.880
will fly Starship's next integrated test.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:24.020
SpaceX rolled ship 40 to the test stand and fired one Raptor completing the vehicle's first

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.120
hot fire test.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.960
The limited duration burn validates initial engine health before a planned multi-engine

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:31.520
campaign.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.580
Ship 40 performed its initial static fire sequence at the orbital launch mount.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.660
The test used a single engine and produced the first visible plume from this particular

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:42.160
vehicle.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.320
Data review will determine when the ship advances to a full six engine firing.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.320
Keep track reports that Ship 40 completed its first engine test while preparations continue

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.440
for a US Starlink mobile service rollout.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.140
The two developments sit on separate timelines, both advanced Starship and Starlink programs.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.700
The US Starlink mobile service rollout remains an active preparation.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.200
Keep track supplied the report on these parallel efforts.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.120
With the upper stage test campaign advancing, attention shifts to the supporting ground infrastructure

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.400
and booster work happening in parallel.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.120
Booster 20 received new hardware at the production site while crews performed fresh work on Starbase

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.180
launch pad 2.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.000
Observers track new hardware visible on booster 20 at the Starbase production site.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:38.080
Photos show fresh structural elements installed since the last public update, consistent with

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.120
ongoing iteration toward the next booster flight test.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:47.560
Ground crews activated Starbase launch pad 2 as part of the ongoing flight 12 test campaign.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.620
The work includes infrastructure checks required before the next full stack attempt.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:59.240
Commenters on NASA's space flight forums highlight the activation of Starbase pad 2 infrastructure.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.940
The discussion centers on how the second pad could eventually support parallel vehicle processing

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.500
once flight 12 clears its test gates.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.900
SpaceX plans a 13 kilometers pipeline.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.740
This pipeline will reduce truck reliance.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.320
The pipeline will speed up Starship rocket launches in the US.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:24.080
Construction of a dedicated 13 kilometers propellant pipeline is slated to begin next month at Starbase.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:29.100
The line is intended to cut truck traffic and shorten turnaround times between launches.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.460
While Starship hardware moves forward, the Falcon program continues its high cadence operations on the

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:41.700
west coast. A Falcon 9 is scheduled to lift off from Vandenberg Space Force Base over the weekend.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:46.800
The mission will deploy another batch of Starlink satellites on a trajectory that may produce

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.540
audible sonic booms along the central California coast. The same Vandenberg Falcon 9 mission is

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:59.200
expected to generate sonic booms audible in parts of Arizona depending on trajectory and weather.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.220
Local authorities have issued standard public advisories for the overflight window.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:11.440
Local Arizona residents discuss expected sonic boom timing for the upcoming Vandenberg Falcon 9.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.740
Comments focus on the precise overflight window and how coastal launch azimuths translate into

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:24.980
inland noise reports. The reliability of SpaceX's workhorse rocket stands in contrast to recent setbacks

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:31.500
elsewhere in the lunar supply chain. A recent blue origin launch vehicle failure has reduced the

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.700
number of certified engines available for NASA's Artemis lunar architecture. The incident increases

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:44.800
reliance on SpaceX hardware for near-term lunar cargo and crew missions. Space enthusiasts note the

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.120
first visible plume from ship 40 and compare it to prior single engine test profiles.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:56.140
Early video analysis suggests the burn duration and plume shape align with the conservative

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:02.900
start planned for new vehicles. RS SpaceX users debate whether Starfall adds value for in-space

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:08.980
manufacturing. Observers note that Starship's own heat shield and catch capability already solve

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:15.620
reentry and recovery. A separate Starfall vehicle appears redundant for returning manufactured goods.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:20.580
The thread questions whether the concept was mainly positioned for investor appeal.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:27.580
The concept may not meet an operational need. Worth keeping an eye on, a blue origin launch vehicle

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:33.740
explosion has removed one certified engine family from near-term NASA lunar missions. Yet it also

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:40.660
underscores that SpaceX still lacks demonstrated orbital refueling at the 15 launch cadence Artemis

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:46.820
planners require. Until Starship completes a full propellant transfer demonstration and rapid booster

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:51.720
reuse, the program remains the single point of failure for the current lunar timeline.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.280
Resolution depends on the next integrated flight test series, achieving those two milestones

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.560
without further slips parallel to launch cadence. SpaceX is also expanding supporting compute

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:11.720
infrastructure. On the AI front Elon Musk SpaceX AI filed $300 million in construction permits

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:17.920
for a Memphis facility expansion tied to the broader Musk compute ecosystem. The permits cover

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:23.640
additional site work at the location already hosting XAI's Colossus class training cluster.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:29.520
From an engineering standpoint, a single engine static fire on a new Starship upper stage

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.840
is the lowest risk way to confirm that the vehicle's propellant feed system, valve timing,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.180
and engine controller all talk to one another before committing six engines to a longer burn.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:46.800
By lighting only one Raptor, engineers isolate any leaks, sensor drift, or software timing issues

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.980
while the vehicle is still bolted to the pad. The physics of cryogenic propellant flow and turbo pump

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:58.960
spin up remain identical to a full duration test, yet the energy released and the potential for

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:05.500
cascading damage stay minimal. Once that first burn succeeds, the test campaign can scale engine

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:10.760
count and duration without repeating the same basic plumbing checks, collapsing the number of

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:16.620
full vehicle rollouts required before flight. The approach mirrors the historical patterns seen in

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:22.960
Falcon 9 engine testing, where incremental firings drove the cost of each additional test down to

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:29.800
the raw propellant and labor floor rather than the cost of an entire stage. The next observable milestone

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:35.860
will be weather ship 40 moves to a three or six engine firing within days, which would indicate

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:41.140
that the single engine data closed the initial control loop questions and cleared the vehicle for

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:50.460
the higher thrust regime needed for flight 12. An quick market note, SPC X closed at $153.23,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:59.120
up 0.3%. Before we go, the next data point to watch is whether ship 40 advances to a multi-engine

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:07.440
static fire in the coming days. That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today. SPC X is trading

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:18.000
at $153.23, up 0.3%. A quick rating on Apple podcasts or Spotify goes a long way. I'm Patrick

